/*
:name: array_vs_packed_dyn
:type: lec
*/
// PYROPE-vs-PYROPE group (README "Pyrope ↔ Pyrope variants"): this file is the
// REFERENCE, and every `array_vs_packed_dyn_<N>.prp` beside it is LEC'd
// against it.
//
// THE CLAIM, in the RUNTIME-index domain: reading entry `raddr` of a `[4]U4`
// register array is `(packed >> (4*raddr)) & 0xf` on the equivalent `u16`.
// This is the read-port half of the array lowering (Ntype_op::Memory `addr`
// pin) rather than the whole-array `read_all` bus the sibling
// array_vs_packed_reg group exercises, so the two groups fail independently.
//
// Deliberately paired with a WHOLE-ARRAY update rather than a per-entry write:
// mixing an `update` bus with per-entry write ports would make a refutation
// ambiguous between the read path and the same-cycle write order.
//
// `= nil` (no reset) ON PURPOSE, so this group states the READ claim and only
// the read claim: it proves from an arbitrary state by flop-cut induction. The
// identical design WITH `= 0` is array_vs_packed_reset, which refutes -- the
// array's reset value is currently dropped. Keeping the two apart is what lets
// this one stay green while that one stays red.
pub mod top(we:U1, v:U16, raddr:U2) -> (rd:U4@[0]) {
  reg foo:[4]U4:[ordering="old"] = nil
  rd = foo[raddr]
  if we != 0 {
    foo = v
  }
}
